On Mar 16, 8:23=A0pm, "!!!@!!!" <eltoni...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hi!
> I'm running Tru64 V5.1A on Personal Alpha Version 2.0.17.
> In DKA0 I have a virtual disk 'disk.vdisk' which I downloaded and
> already contains the Tru64 system installed.
> In DKA100 I have selected an ISO file which is non-bootable. It's just
> an Image of a Data CD with some files I want to open from Tru64.
> How do I mount the ISO and open it's contents?
> Which of the devices is DKA100 in Tru64?
> My OS is Windows XP SP3.
>
> Elton

Umm, your explanation of the Personal ALpha configuration is difficult
to understand.
In the Personal Alpha configuration panel there are four disks
available: DKA0 .. DKA300.
The DKA names are the emulated hardware devices: a real Alpha would
show these same names when you type the SHOW DEVICES command on the
>>> prompt.
On an emulated system there are no real disks but files on the host
system (in your case a pc with Windows XP sp 3).
By convention Stromasys uses the filetype .vdisk but you're free to
give the file any name you'd like.
I understand that you've been succesful in booting Tru64 from DKA0,
right?
If so then DKA100 will show up in the directory as /dev/disk/dsk1c
(assuming that DKA0 is called /dev/disk/dsk0c).
Perhaps Personal Alpha is configured that the .iso file is actually an
emulated cdrom, in which case the the device is called /dev/disk/
cdrom0c
That would make sense since you can't destroy that file.
Try mount -q /dev/disk/cdrom0c to check whether Tru64 recognizes the
contents of the .iso file.
When it does, it will suggest the correct filesystem to mount it as.
Hans


