The boot flags may be passed to the OS from the srm commandline.
So this would be a valid alternative:

>>> b -fl A dka0

The srm commandline overrides the stored option values.
It seems the emulator sees dka100 as a cdrom device.
Apparently your base system wasn't configured with a cdrom.
You write that mount -q 'resulted in an error, possibly because you
targeted it at /dev/disk/dsk1c right?
You'd have to add a cdrom device to your emulated system. Which is
where my poor understanding of Tru64 ends I'm afraid.
Hans


