You can always test what kind of device or file you have behind the 
device file by issuing the command:

# file /dev/rdisk/dsk1a (or whatever id you have)

Could be /dev/disk/cdrom0c also, like Hans mentioned.

In a case of a real hardware device, you should get a short description 
of the device. If it is a filesystem file, you should get an 
approximation what kind of file it is.

When mounting ISO files you usually use the option cdfs; like follows:

# mount -r -t cdfs /dev/disk/dsk1c

Depending on the ISO image, you might also need the option rrip:

# mount -r -t cdfs -o rrip /dev/disk/dsk1c


Kari



