Good that you got it working.

The last letter of the device name e.g. floppy2c <----
is a way of defining a partition (or slice as it is called in Solaris) 
on a disk. By definition the c partition covers the whole disk and the a 
partition is the first one from beginning of the disk, which usually is 
used for booting the OS. The rest of the partitions (b,d,e,f,g and h) 
can be configured as the user wishes. In case of a floppy or CD-ROM both 
a and c works similarly unless you have partitioned them and the a 
partition is smaller than the disk.

It is hard to answer the question about multiple floppies without 
knowing how the emulator is configured. Could be that the device type is 
defined as a floppy. There used to be SCSI floppy drives.
A real AlphaServer might have two IDE buses with two possible device 
connections each. Maybe the emulator thinks the IDE buses of your 
computer are potential floppy drive attachment points. This is pure 
guesswork, of course. Does the emulator documentation shed any light on 
the issue?


Kari




