Kari,
I think that the Personal Alpha emulator was configured without a
cdrom device.
And there may be no need for a cdrom.  There is hardly any difference
between a disk and a cdrom other than that the last is of course
readonly.
But for an emulator both a disk and a cdrom have the same
representation: a file on a disk of the hostsystem. The file that
represents a cd may be set readonly but there the differences end. Now
the on-disk structure may differ of course between a disk and a cd.
Unix is pretty good at that, it has different choices for filesystems
on a regular harddisk even.
Floppies use a different filesystem altogether IIRC but the mount
command is flexible: it allows you to mount various filesystems on all
